### Tool Class — `WeatherTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetCurrentWeather(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'London' or 'New York'")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching weather for {City}", city);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("weather");
        var response = await client.GetStringAsync(
            $"/v1/current.json?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a multi-day weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        [Description("Number of forecast days (1-7)")] int days = 3,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("weather");
        var response = await client.GetStringAsync(
            $"/v1/forecast.json?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&days={days}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

### `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("weather", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weatherapi.com");
    client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Accept", "application/json");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

### How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | How it works |
|---|---|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | Services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>` are injected via the primary constructor. The class is resolved from the DI container per invocation. |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services appear as method parameters alongside tool parameters. The SDK resolves them automatically. |

**Key rule:** The SDK distinguishes tool parameters from DI services automatically. Only parameters with `[Description]` appear in the tool's JSON schema exposed to the LLM. Types like `CancellationToken`, `ILogger<T>`, `HttpClient`, `IMcpServer`, and any DI-registered service are **injected silently** — the LLM never sees them.

In the example above, `IHttpClientFactory` and `ILogger<WeatherTools>` are constructor-injected because the class is non-static. `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` discovers the `[McpServerToolType]` class and registers it automatically.
